@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@using System.Globalization
@using SpaceSails.Client.Rendering
@using SpaceSails.Contracts
@using SpaceSails.Core
@using SpaceSails.Core.Interior
@*
    #251 item 1 · THE JUMP CARD, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    Moved out of Map.razor by PURE MOTION — the block between the two fences below is the block that was in
    the page, character for character. Every page member it reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME NAME (the
    fields keep their underscore, the methods come across as delegates), which is what lets the markup move
    without a rewrite and lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as before.

    The `@if` that opens it stays in Map.razor: the page keeps its paint order in one place, and a closed
    surface still renders nothing. IHandleEvent repeats the page's own override (Map.Sim.cs) — without it
    Blazor would put an automatic re-render back on every event in here, which the page turns off because
    the HUD's refresh belongs to OnTick's 200 ms throttle. The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules;
    it exists to give the component Map.razor.css's scope (#251 item 3 moves the rules).

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block used to sit inside `@if (…) { … }`,
    which is a Razor CODE block, and a code block is where its first statements are legal. The braces
    put the moved lines back in the context they were written in; they are outside the fences, so what
    the source guards read is still exactly the lines that were in the page.
*@
@* ── THE PAGE'S OWN NOTE ON THIS SURFACE, moved here with it (#251 item 1) and not touched by a
   word: it is the paragraph that stood above this block in Map.razor. It sits ABOVE the fence, not
   inside it, so that a source guard slicing the block by text goes on reading markup and not prose.
   ── *@
    @* #255 — the diegetic long-haul jump overlay: the one loading bar allowed to be a FEATURE. While
       the world re-seeds at the arrival epoch, the screen SAYS the state — CROSSING THE VOID, the years
       ticking up, and a charming note that the bus does not stop out here. No cancel is offered.

       #992 · …and no cancel is offered STILL, because cancelling a crossing is a decision about the world
       and the tick loop is frozen behind _jumpInProgress so the void is never integrated. What #992 added is
       a decision about the SCREEN: the sheet tucks into a corner tile and the map comes back, while the
       crossing runs on exactly the clock it always ran on.

       #997 wave 2 · …AND THE GESTURE IS NOT ITS OWN ANY MORE. #992 hand-rolled that minimise, which made it
       the THIRD independent implementation of one idea after #963's scope and #960's dossier: a third gate
       written into the open card's @@if, a third bespoke – button, and a tile drawn as a SEPARATE sibling
       element that had to be kept agreeing with the sheet about which year it is. All three are gone. The
       shell owns the gesture; the tile is the SAME element with its class list swapped, so sheet and tile
       cannot disagree because there is only one of them; and _voidCardTucked is now only what the page
       REMEMBERS rather than a thing it also has to draw. The sheet keeps its own class, its own geometry
       and its own words, and it still offers no cancel — that decision is about the world, not the screen. *@

@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from Map.razor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <OverlayShell class="jump-overlay"
                      Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Minimize" @bind-Minimized="_voidCardTucked"
                      HeadClass="jump-overlay-head" BodyClass="jump-overlay-inner"
                      DismissClass="jump-overlay-min" DismissFace="–"
                      DismissLabel="Minimise the crossing"
                      DismissTitle="Tuck the crossing into a tile — it keeps its own clock and the map comes back"
                      TileClass="jump-overlay-tile" TileGlyph="🚀"
                      TileLabel="@LongHaul.VoidYearLine(_jumpYear, _jumpTotalYears)"
                      TileButtonClass="btn btn-sm btn-outline-info"
                      OpenTitle="Open the crossing back up">
            <div class="jump-overlay-title">🚀 @LongHaul.VoidTitle</div>
            <div class="jump-overlay-year">@LongHaul.VoidYearLine(_jumpYear, _jumpTotalYears)</div>
            <div class="jump-overlay-bar">
                <div class="jump-overlay-fill" style="width:@(_jumpTotalYears > 0 ? (int)(100.0 * Math.Clamp(_jumpYear, 0, _jumpTotalYears) / _jumpTotalYears) : 100)%"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="jump-overlay-bound">@LongHaul.VoidBound(_jumpDestName)</div>
            <div class="jump-overlay-note">@_jumpFlavor</div>
            <div class="jump-overlay-nostop">@LongHaul.VoidNoStop</div>
        </OverlayShell>
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}

@code {
    // ── WHAT THESE PARAMETERS ARE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // #251 item 1 · Every [Parameter] below carries a member of the page under THE MEMBER'S OWN NAME —
    // a field keeps its underscore, a method arrives as a delegate with its own signature, a variable the
    // page's `@if` binds arrives under the name the `@if` gave it. That is the whole trick of this
    // refactor: it is what let the markup above move out of Map.razor without a single character of it
    // changing, and what lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as they read it
    // when it lived in the page. A parameter that needs saying more than that says it on its own line.

    [Parameter] public string _jumpDestName { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public string _jumpFlavor { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public int _jumpTotalYears { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public int _jumpYear { get; set; }
    /// <summary>the page's `bool _voidCardTucked` — and this markup WRITES it, so it crosses as a pair: the
    /// value in, the page's own setter out. The private property below keeps the member's own name, so
    /// the moved block still assigns to `_voidCardTucked` and the assignment still lands on the page.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool _voidCardTuckedValue { get; set; }
    /// <summary>The page's `_voidCardTucked = value`, handed down so the write survives the move.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Action<bool> _voidCardTuckedSet { get; set; } = default!;
    private bool _voidCardTucked { get => _voidCardTuckedValue; set { _voidCardTuckedValue = value; _voidCardTuckedSet(value); } }
    [Parameter] public Func<Action, Task> Dismiss { get; set; } = default!;

    // The page's own event dispatch, repeated: no automatic re-render per event.
    Task IHandleEvent.HandleEventAsync(EventCallbackWorkItem callback, object? arg) => callback.InvokeAsync(arg);
}
